::STAT_U06_L01_GP_Q01::
Question ID: STAT_U06_L01_GP_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.PSU.11
Question:
A student rolls one fair number cube. Let X be the number rolled. What is X?
{
=A. A random variable#X assigns a numerical value to the outcome of the roll.
~B. A probability#A probability describes chance. X is the number assigned to an outcome.
~C. A sample space#The sample space is the set of possible outcomes, not the variable itself.
~D. A graph#A graph can show the distribution, but X is not the graph.
}

::STAT_U06_L01_GP_Q02::
Question ID: STAT_U06_L01_GP_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.PSU.11
Question:
If X is the number of heads when two coins are tossed, which list shows all possible values of X?
{
=A. 0, 1, 2#With two coins, the number of heads can be 0, 1, or 2.
~B. Heads, tails#Those are coin outcomes, not numerical values of the random variable.
~C. 1, 2#This misses the possible value 0 when no heads occur.
~D. 0, 1, 2, 3#Three heads is not possible when only two coins are tossed.
}

::STAT_U06_L01_GP_Q03::
Question ID: STAT_U06_L01_GP_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.PSU.11
Question:
A probability distribution has probabilities 0.20, 0.50, and 0.30. Is it valid?
{
~A. No, because 0.50 is too large#A probability of 0.50 is valid because it is between 0 and 1.
=B. Yes, because each probability is between 0 and 1 and the total is 1#Both validity rules are satisfied.
~C. No, because there are three values#A distribution may have three values; the probabilities must be checked.
~D. Yes, because the largest probability is listed in the middle#The position of the largest probability does not determine validity.
}

::STAT_U06_L01_GP_Q04::
Question ID: STAT_U06_L01_GP_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.PSU.11
Question:
Which probability list cannot be a valid probability distribution?
{
~A. 0.25, 0.25, 0.50#These probabilities are between 0 and 1 and add to 1.
~B. 0.10, 0.40, 0.50#These probabilities are between 0 and 1 and add to 1.
~C. 0.30, 0.30, 0.40#These probabilities are between 0 and 1 and add to 1.
=D. 0.20, 0.60, 0.40#These add to 1.20, which is more than 1.
}

::STAT_U06_L01_GP_Q05::
Question ID: STAT_U06_L01_GP_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.STAT.PSU.11
Question:
A bar graph of a probability distribution has the tallest bar at X = 3. What does that mean?
{
~A. X = 3 must happen every time#The most likely value is not guaranteed in a random process.
~B. X = 3 has probability 1#A tallest bar does not automatically mean the probability is 1.
~C. X = 3 is the only possible value#Other bars may still represent other possible values.
=D. X = 3 is the most likely value#The tallest probability bar represents the value with the greatest probability.
}
